<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Genius Innovations – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Entrepreneurial Spirit</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Genius Innovations</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reviews on general products or services. Interactive: Collaborate on creating guidelines for group work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write reviews of business innovations and ideas, evaluating their benefits for efficiency (e.g., "This app helps track sales, which can improve customer service.")
Can collaborate on creating business guidelines identifying product marketing strategies (e.g., "Our guidelines should suggest social media advertising to attract customers.")
Can develop a collaborative guide for planning a business, assigning sections to each team member (e.g., "I'll be analyzing the market trends, and you will be preparing the social media campaigns.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of various innovative products or services (e.g., a smart home device, a new educational app, an eco-friendly transport solution). Students individually write down 2-3 adjectives to describe each innovation and then share one with a partner, explaining why they chose it. This activates vocabulary like <i>innovative, efficient, advanced, unique, customer-focused</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple positive review for one of the displayed innovations. For example, "This new app is incredibly <i>efficient</i> for tracking daily tasks. If you use it, you will <i>improve</i> your productivity. Our team has recently <i>implemented</i> it, and it's a great <i>innovation</i>." The teacher explicitly highlights how to structure a brief review (introduction, benefit, call to action/recommendation) and pronounces key multisyllabic words like "efficient" /ɪˈfɪʃ.ənt/ and "implemented" /ɪmˈplɪ.mən.tɪd/ for students to notice reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are the main parts of a product review?" (Expected answer: "Introduction, benefits, recommendation.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'efficient,' what does that mean the product does?" (Expected answer: "It helps things work well without wasting time or resources.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with two short, authentic online reviews of business-related apps or services (e.g., project management software, a marketing automation tool). One review should be positive, highlighting benefits for <i>efficiency</i> and <i>customer engagement</i>, and the other should be constructive, pointing out areas for <i>improvement</i> or <i>reduction</i> of <i>costs</i>. The texts explicitly feature First Conditional and Present Perfect for innovations, along with target vocabulary like <i>strategy, marketing, customer base, technology, launch</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reviews on general products or services. Interactive: Collaborate on creating guidelines for group work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the two reviews individually and identify the main innovation being reviewed in each. (Gist)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements about the reviews' content. For example: "The first review suggests that if a business <i>implements</i> this app, they will <i>reduce costs</i>." "The second review mentions that the company <i>has launched</i> several <i>promotional events</i> to <i>increase visibility</i>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match phrases from the reviews that use the First Conditional (e.g., "If we use social media, we can reach a larger audience.") or Present Perfect for innovations (e.g., "Local shops have begun using social media to tell more people about their products.") to their corresponding outcomes or descriptions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice constructing sentences using the First Conditional, Present Perfect for innovations, and Future Continuous, incorporating target vocabulary related to <i>innovation, business, strategy, marketing, customer base, efficiency, launch, collaborate, promote, establish</i>. Pronunciation practice of /ɔː/ in words like "launch," "cost," "talk" will be integrated by encouraging students to read their sentences aloud.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete sentences. They must choose the correct form of a verb and a vocabulary word to complete sentences, practicing First Conditional and Present Perfect for innovations. (e.g., "If we ___ (invest) in new ___ (technology), we ___ (increase) our ___ (customer base)." / "Our start-up ___ (develop) a highly ___ (efficient) ___ (app) for ___ (automation) in the past year.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Review Sentence Building):</b> In pairs, students select three innovative products or services discussed in previous lessons. For each, they collaboratively write three sentences: one describing a benefit using First Conditional (e.g., "If this app helps track sales, it will definitely <i>improve customer service</i>."), one describing its impact using Present Perfect (e.g., "This feature <i>has enhanced</i> our marketing <i>strategy</i>."), and one predicting a future action using Future Continuous (e.g., "Next month, I'll be <i>analyzing the market trends</i> for this product line."). They are encouraged to use professional language for business ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Guideline Statements:</b> Still in pairs, students imagine they are creating guidelines for a new collaborative business project. They write 2-3 guideline statements using First Conditional or Future Continuous, focusing on roles or strategies. (e.g., "If we <i>collaborate</i> effectively, we will <i>succeed</i> in our <i>mission</i>." / "I'll be <i>preparing the social media campaigns</i>, and you'll be <i>developing</i> the <i>product line</i>."). They peer-check each other's sentences for grammatical accuracy and appropriate vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Entrepreneurial Spirit")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4) to complete one of two writing tasks, directly connected to the theme "My Genius Innovations" and the scenario "Entrepreneurial Spirit."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Written Product (Review or Guidelines)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A (Product Review):</b> Each group chooses one of the "genius innovations" discussed in the warm-up or previous lessons. They collaboratively write a review (100-120 words) that evaluates its benefits for <i>efficiency</i> and potential for growth. The review must include at least two instances of the First Conditional, two of the Present Perfect for innovations, and incorporate at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>innovation, strategy, customer engagement, marketing, automated</i>). They should use professional language and clearly share ideas for innovation, respectfully integrating each other's suggestions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B (Business Guidelines):</b> Groups collaborate to create a section of "Business Guidelines for Launching an Innovative Product." They must outline 3-4 key strategies for their chosen imaginary product, such as <i>marketing, customer base engagement</i>, or <i>cost reduction</i>. The guidelines should assign sections to each team member (e.g., "I'll be analyzing the market trends, and you will be preparing the social media campaigns.") and use First Conditional to describe expected outcomes (e.g., "If we <i>promote</i> this effectively, we will <i>increase visibility</i> and <i>demand</i>."). They must include at least two instances of the First Conditional, two of the Future Continuous, and 5-7 target vocabulary words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting students to use target structures, and facilitating collaboration, especially on how to respectfully incorporate each other's ideas (sociolinguistic competence).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment based on a simplified rubric for the written output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their collaborative written product (review or guidelines). Before submission, groups exchange their written work with another group for peer review using a checklist focused on the use of target grammar and vocabulary, clarity, and adherence to the task requirements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Assessment):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear use of First Conditional (2+ instances)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear use of Present Perfect for innovations (2+ instances, for reviews) OR Future Continuous (2+ instances, for guidelines)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (nouns, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the written text (review evaluation or guideline strategies).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates collaborative effort and professional language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall quality of written expression at B1.2 level.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students individually complete a short reflection:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One specific strategy I used to make my writing clearer was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did my group use professional language to share our ideas for innovation?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing this review/these guidelines, and how did I overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new vocabulary word or grammar structure I feel more confident using in my writing now?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? The students' engagement in collaborative writing and their application of the First Conditional and Present Perfect for innovations was strong. The peer-assessment activity encouraged critical review of written work.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some groups struggled with integrating Future Continuous naturally into their guidelines. For future writing tasks, more explicit modeling of sentence structures for predictions and future plans might be beneficial. Perhaps provide sentence starters for future continuous.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This writing lesson on creating reviews and guidelines directly prepares students for Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration). In the next lesson, students will consolidate all their learning by summarizing and synthesizing information about "My Genius Innovations" to present their own innovative business idea, which will involve integrating information from their written reviews/guidelines, and potentially mediating ideas to a wider audience or creating a more comprehensive written report for their 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>